# Programs for a real OS-9 system that this emulator cannot exercise:
# drivers and file managers, the MM/1 and X68000, multi-user and modem
# lines, OS-9/Net.  Each card shows what can be shown here -- a module's
# header, a usage line, the refusal -- and says plainly it is untested.

shot    ptyman
for     ptyman ptydrv pty
cap     PtyMan is a file manager: pairs of pseudo-terminals, /pty/<name>
cap     for the program that plays the terminal and /tty/<name> for the
cap     one that thinks it has one.  We cannot test it -- the emulator
cap     loads no file managers -- so each module's header is what there is
cap     to show: the file manager, its driver, and its two descriptors.
try     modinfo ptyman ptydrv pty tty
run     builtin cd /dd/CMDS/DRIVERS
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     modinfo ptyman ptydrv pty tty
wait    4

shot    lfcrman
for     lfcrman h0t thlpct
cap     A file manager that shows another device with LF and CR swapped,
cap     for text shared with Unix over NFS; h0t and thlpct are its example
cap     descriptors.  Untested here: the emulator loads no file managers.
try     modinfo lfcrman h0t thlpct
run     builtin cd /dd/CMDS/DRIVERS
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     modinfo lfcrman h0t thlpct
wait    4

shot    disp
for     disp di
cap     The C device driver from OS-9 International 1/93, with its
cap     descriptor di: a worked example of writing a driver in C, four
cap     bits to a display port.  Untested: it wants the port.
try     modinfo disp di
run     builtin cd /dd/CMDS/DRIVERS
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     modinfo disp di
wait    4

shot    cpucache
for     cpucache cctrap
cap     cpucache shows and switches a 68020, 68030 or 68040's on-chip
cap     caches, through its trap handler cctrap.  Its usage is all that
cap     can be shown: the emulator has no caches to switch, and the
cap     package's own driver is not here.
try     cpucache -?
run     clear
run     cpucache -?
wait    3

shot    vt100
cap     A terminal program for the MM/1: VT-100 and VT-52 emulation in a
cap     K-Windows window.  Here it has no serial port to open, and says so.
try     vt100 /t2
run     clear
run     vt100 /t2 < /nil
wait    3

shot    x9eyes
for     x9eyes setbgptn
cap     Two programs for the Sharp X68000's personal window: x9eyes, eyes
cap     that follow the pointer, and setbgptn, the window system's
cap     background pattern.  Without the X68000's /win each stops before
cap     drawing anything, and says why.
try     x9eyes
run     clear
run     x9eyes
wait    3
run     setbgptn SYS/motd
wait    3

shot    logon
for     logon timeout
cap     logon logs a user into a multi-user OS-9: password file, user,
cap     environment and motd.  Its usage is its manual's summary; the
cap     manual is DOC/SYSADMIN/logon.prf.  timeout is its idle timer.
try     logon -?
run     clear
run     logon -?
wait    3

shot    mmon
for     mmon watch
size    48 80
cap     mmon, a timesharing monitor for a terminal or modem line: it
cap     answers, sets the speed and runs logon.  watch waits for a modem to
cap     ring and starts a command -- for the super-user, and it tells
cap     anyone else so.
try     mmon -?
run     clear
run     mmon -?
wait    3
run     watch -?
wait    3

shot    errlog
for     errlog erradm
cap     errlog gathers error messages from other programs into one log;
cap     erradm starts, stops, flushes and prints it.
try     erradm -?
run     clear
run     errlog -?
wait    2
run     erradm -?
wait    3

shot    vcron
for     vcron crontab
cap     Vixie's cron.  vcron runs only for members of the group Cron,
cap     which this disk has not got, so it says so; crontab lists,
cap     installs, edits and removes a user's own table.
try     crontab -l
run     clear
run     vcron
wait    3
run     crontab -l
wait    3

shot    rex
for     rex chp remdate prexd prexdc
cap     rex runs a command on another OS-9/Net node; chp logs in to one;
cap     remdate takes its clock from one; prexd and prexdc are the daemon
cap     and server on the node that answers.  Without a network each asks
cap     for /n0 and stops.
try     rex -?
run     clear
run     rex -?
wait    3
run     remdate
wait    3

shot    uid
for     uid newgrp
cap     uid prints a user's ids from the password file -- here the tester
cap     account this disk carries; the group shows as UNKNOWN because there
cap     is no SYS/group.  newgrp changes your group, as SYS/group allows.
try     uid tester
run     clear
run     uid tester
wait    2
run     newgrp -?
wait    3

shot    mesg
for     mesg msg speak
cap     Messages between users logged in at once, whom logon records in
cap     SYS/utmp: mesg sets whether you take them, msg sends a line, speak
cap     opens a split-screen chat.  Here nobody else is logged in, so
cap     their usage and speak's refusal are what can be shown.
try     speak tester
run     clear
run     mesg -?
wait    2
run     speak tester
wait    3

shot    mmenu
for     mmenu mwb
cap     mmenu gives users a menu instead of a shell, as on a BBS, read from
cap     menu files; mwb writes manual entries in TOP's proff format.  With
cap     no menus or manual directory here, their usage is what shows.
try     mmenu
run     clear
run     mmenu
wait    3
run     mwb -?
wait    3
